{
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "beat-1-force",
      "text": "When a rocket booster falls back to Earth, gravity pulls it down at extreme speeds. To slow it down, an external force must act in the opposite direction of its motion."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-2-propulsion",
      "text": "A rocket creates this braking force by expelling onboard mass. Chemical combustion heats gases that expand and shoot out of the nozzle, pushing the vehicle in the opposite direction."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-3-descent",
      "text": "During descent, the Falcon 9 first-stage restarts engines for an entry burn to begin slowing down, while grid fins aerodynamically steer its descent."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-4-braking",
      "text": "Finally, a landing engine ignition fires. This downward exhaust provides the critical upward thrust that halts the booster's downward speed, achieving a soft vertical landing."
    }
  ],
